obe/budget.py:4-5`\n**Issue:** The function is a plain addition and works for any float, but the result is only meaningful if the caller compares it against the same clock that produced `start_secs`. A caller passing `time.time()` and later comparing against `time.monotonic()` would get a wrong deadline, with no error. There is no docstring to say which is expected. No caller exists yet, so nothing is broken today.\n**Fix:** Add a one-line docstring, for example: `\"\"\"Return the deadline for start_secs; compare it against the same clock (prefer time.monotonic()).\"\"\"`\n\n### IN-02: Constant is not marked `Final`\n\n**File:** `probe/budget.py:1`\n**Issue:** `DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS: int = 120` can be rebound by any importer, and `deadline_after` reads the global at call time, so a rebinding changes behaviour process-wide. Type checkers will not flag it.\n**Fix:** `from typing import Final` then `DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS: Final[int] = 120`. Tests that monkeypatch it would still work at runtime.\n\n### IN-03: The existing test cannot detect a wrong budget choice\n\n**File:** `tests/test_budget.py:5-10` (context for `probe/budget.py:1`)\n**Issue:** The test accepts any of 30, 120 or 300. The plan's own rationale says 30 and 300 are the wrong answers (a 30 s value fails every 90 s failover; 300 s outlasts the 240 s pager). A regression to either would still pass. This matters for reliability of the check, not for the current code.\n**Fix:** Optionally add an assertion that the value lies strictly between the 90 s failover window and the 240 s pager threshold, with those two numbers as named constants.\n\n---\n\n_Reviewed: 2026-10-21_\n_Reviewer: Claude (gsd-code-reviewer)_\n_Depth: standard_\n=== 01-VALIDATION.md\n---\nphase: \"01\"\nslug: \"retry-budget\"\nstatus: validated\nnyquist_compliant: true\nwave_0_complete: true\ncreated: \"2026-09-28\"\n---\n\n# Phase 01 — Validation Strategy\n\n> Per-phase validation contract for feedback sampling during execution.\n\n